  "protocol": "Four Orders near-tie decision confirmation, 23 November 2026\nOwner request: ricardofitas/rig-comm issue 1 comment 7105600256.\n\nChoose Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct versus Granite-3.3-2B-Instruct, Q4_K_M.\nIn the opened first 288 SciQ and ARC-Easy items both model accuracies\nexceed chance on each complete 480-item panel; the first-288 gaps are\n4.86 and 3.18 percentage points. No selection on confirmation outcomes.\nPin the physical RTX 4060 Ti, local llama.cpp b12631 transport and\nexisting eight-slot native 512-token decoder. Require zero changes across\ntwo cold replays of 384 model/item/order choices before launching.\n\nUse ARC-Challenge validation and OpenBookQA main validation. Freeze all\n288 exact rows and fixture digests for each before GPU admission. ARC\nhas 292 label-blind candidates. Equal panel size is the largest full\neight-item shard count supported by the tighter panel. Deduplicate exact\nnormalized stems within and across panels and against the opened SciQ,\nARC-Easy, OpenBookQA test and historical ARC source metadata. Model\ntraining overlap is unknown. No reopening earlier reserved panels.\nFor both panels use the standard zero-shot numeric prompt and all 24 orders\nper model. Use same independently sampled shared block for both models\nper item, independent blocks across items. Decision is whether Qwen leads\nby at least 5 percentage points, counting equality as a lead. Use the\n24-order paired accuracy gap as reference. Keep both panels even if far\nfrom the chosen boundary. Retain all checkpointed choices.\n\nPrimary effect is precedence-minimax error probability minus Williams\nerror probability, evaluated at the same four calls per model and item.\nAn absolute improvement of more than 1 percentage point is practically\nmeaningful, corresponding to one fewer incorrect decision per 100 repetitions.\nThe opened 288-item development checks gave absolute gains of +0.040 and\n-0.008 percentage points with directional bootstrap ranges (-0.401, 0.583)\nand (-0.396, 0.322). These are sizing sensitivity checks, not guaranteed\nconfirmation power. Reference classification can be unstable at the boundary.\n\nUse direct numerical convolution for the finite-panel probability and\ncross-check by FFT to 1e-9. For 4,000 paired bootstrap item resamples\nrecompute the reference decision and both errors, with seeded FFT, and\nuse percentile 2.5th/97.5th bounds. These are simultaneous directional\nbounds at 5% across two panels separately for each direction. Pass if\nboth lower bounds exceed +0.01; rule out the joint practical claim if\neither upper bound falls below +0.01; otherwise inconclusive. Report when\nbootstrap reference decisions change. The tail convolution does not use\nMonte Carlo. Secondary baseline ordinary cyclic; all-design paired-gap\nMSE is secondary. Seal official answers until both GPU releases are\ncomplete; interrupted runs resume committed predictions or remain unscored.\n",
